= Flash memory — Simple =
<onlyinclude>'''Flash memory''' is a kind of electronic memory that keeps stored data when power is switched off. It is used in memory cards, USB drives, phones, SSDs and some device firmware. Unlike ordinary working memory (DRAM), it does not need regular refresh to keep its bits.
== How can it remember without power? ==
Tiny semiconductor structures hold electrical charge in insulated regions. The charge changes how a cell behaves when read. The device can change that charge to store new information, but writing and erasing are not as flexible as changing a byte in RAM.
== NAND and NOR ==
NAND flash is common in high-capacity storage. It often writes data in pages and erases larger blocks. NOR flash is often used where software needs to read code at directly addressable locations. Neither label tells you the speed or lifespan of every product.<ref name="hist">Kioxia, [https://www.kioxia.com/en-jp/rd/technology/history.html technology history], including early flash and NAND milestones.</ref>
== Why can flash wear out? ==
Repeated programming and erasing stress the storage structures. Charge can also leak over time. A controller may correct some bit errors, avoid defective blocks and spread writes across usable blocks. These safeguards reduce risk but do not make data permanent.
== Why can deleting a file be complicated? ==
A NAND device may need to erase a whole block before reusing part of it. A storage controller can copy still-needed data elsewhere and erase obsolete blocks later. The computer's file-delete command, the controller's mapping changes and physical erasure are different events. This matters for data recovery, privacy and performance.
== What does a standard guarantee? ==
An interface standard defines how compatible devices and controllers communicate; it does not promise that every flash chip has identical endurance, retention or power-loss protection.<ref name="onfisimple">Open NAND Flash Interface Workgroup, [https://onfi.org/files/ONFI_6_0_Final.pdf ONFI 6.0 (18 December 2025)], NAND interface specification.</ref>

